Attempted to prove c5InterpolationWeightSum 1 < 10^11 by expanding the
129-node derivative-product formula and normalizing the resulting rational
sum. The build command was:

SIGMA_REFERENCE_ROOT=/home/thlinux/worktrees/geometry-of-numbers-sigma-allorder-negative-certified-20261105 \
CARRY_LEAN_ROOT=/home/thlinux/worktrees/carry-sigma-c2-pinned-045369 \
SIGMA_OUTPUT_DIR=/home/thlinux/worktrees/geometry-of-numbers-c5-finite-c2-certification/.lake/build/research/native-moving-schur-sigma/c5-weight-degree1-numeric \
bash research/native-moving-schur-sigma/compile_sigma_pade.sh SigmaRationalInterpolation

With maxRecDepth=10000, maxHeartbeats=0 and simp maxSteps=500000, Lean 4.32
ran for more than ten minutes and returned:

research/native-moving-schur-sigma/SigmaRationalInterpolation.lean:80:2:
error: `simp` failed: maximum number of steps exceeded

An earlier run with the default simp step limit returned the same error. The
numeric theorem was removed. The exact derivative formula and coefficient-one
identity were retained because they compile independently. This is a
simplifier resource limit; it does not show that the claimed inequality is
false. Resume with a more structural rational bound instead of expanding all
129 products at once.
